A||N||12||burdell1||Sticky Door Lock||||5||
Z||000000||burdell1||12-30-1999||06:21 PM||||I've been having problems with my power door lock on the inside of the passenger side door. When I press the unlock button on my remote (I have keyless entry), the lock won't slide all the way over to unlock it. Awhile back I had this problem on both sides, but put a bolt and washer next to the wire that unlocks and locks it to give it more ooph and its worked fine since then. However, when it gets colder the door locks still sticks and doesn't want to open. I tried greasing it, graphite and spraying a ton of WD-40 on it, but to no avail.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-30-1999||08:03 PM||||Try a voltage meter at the actuator.  Maybe it's not getting the right amount of juice.  Or maybe it's just time for new actuators.||||reg||1
Z||000002||burdell1||12-30-1999||08:10 PM||||where is the actuator at? Does it cost much to replace?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||12-30-1999||08:56 PM||||Lubing the slide knob wont help you need to get inside the door and lube all the linkage||||reg||1
Z||000004||burdell1||12-30-1999||09:26 PM||||I did that too.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||TOM||12-30-1999||09:52 PM||||The actuators may be going out ot have rust on the pins. They are located in the rear part of the door mounted up and down behind the armrest support||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ed||12-30-1999||11:19 PM||||I read that it was "a piece of cake" to take off the inner door panels to get at the locks. Is it? The shop manual wants you to use a special tool to pry off 8 or 9 fasteners. <P>If its easy I'd go in there and lube my power locks too, but what are we getting into? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||12-31-1999||12:44 AM||||The tool is a fork looking thing that helps pull the fasteners without breaking them. You could use a flat screwdriver or two but the tool is like $3 at a local parts store||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ed||12-31-1999||01:38 AM||||So, is it "a piece of cake?"<P>Will the counterperson know what a "fork looking thing" for removing door panels is, or is there a technical term for it besides GM # J-24595B? <P>When I break the fasteners, can they be replaced or glued?<P>This from someone who just took over five hours to do a front brake pad and rotor replacement job (although the brakes work and the wheels haven't fallen off yet).  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||12-31-1999||05:01 AM||||It's a piece of cake for sure!  Nothing could be simpler.  Remove armrest (3 screws), pop off the 2 screw covers on the handle trim (1 is round/behind the handle, other is rectangular with the Pegasus embossed in it) and remove the trim (it slides off to the front-remember to pull the wires from the p/lock switch if you have them), remove the window crank (for manual windows) and remember to remove the manual mirror joystick on the drivers side (if this applies-open window and there's a set screw on the trim piece).  Most of the fasteners are around the edge of the panel, however...there are 2 (maybe only 1-I forget) fasteners farther in the panel.  One is top front in about 3 to 4 inches.<P>You can buy more fasteners from your local parts house very cheaply.  They are generic.  Don't bother trying to glue them.<P>Last (whew!), just tell the counterperson that you need a tool to remove an inner door panel.  They'll know exactly what you need.  If they don't, run (don't walk) from the store and never return!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Hope this helps.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 12-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ed||12-31-1999||09:14 PM||||It was a piece of cake.<P>Special thanks to Tom and Monkeyman.<P>The forked tool cost $10 instead of $3. For anybody else doing this exercise: Judicious use of a flat bladed screwdriver would probably pop the fasteners out, although it was nice "using the right tool for the job." <P>The shop manual illustration showed nine fasteners, but there were only eight. They were all around the edge of the trim, none set back. 87 GT if it matters. <P>Well, I think that about does it for my restoration. I also put in an original equipment (GM) trunk weatherstrip today. The paint's good, the CD player and new front speakers are good, the engine's strong, the clutch has been replaced (didn't fail, camshaft oil seal failed and oiled the disc), pretty much all the engine rubber has been replaced, front pads and rotors replaced, engine tuned up, and a new millenium is upon us.<P>HAPPY NEW YEAR  <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 12-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||01-01-2000||04:30 AM||||Once more about the new millenium...no, it's not. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ed||01-01-2000||03:51 PM||||It doesn't matter. There's no such thing as time anyway.<P>Now, back to important things like cars.||||reg||1
